@node	5.5, References, 5.4, Chapter 5
@section Compilation

The explicit-control evaluator of @ref{5.4} interprets checked programs in
the OCaml guest subset.  This section studies the other execution path: the
compiler translates the same checked syntax into typed register-machine
instructions, which the 5.1 simulator executes.

The guest language is the finite OCaml subset defined in
@code{spec/@/host-subsets/@/ocaml/@/grammar.md}.  Admission parses and
type-checks each complete source unit before an evaluator runs it.  The
compiler consumes that checked AST; it does not lower guest syntax into
another language or treat rendered instructions as source.  A suitable
instruction sequence can express a computation in this subset.  This is
not a claim that the simulator is a general-purpose high-performance CPU.

The public interfaces mark the boundary.  @code{Check.check} admits source.
@code{Sec_5_5.compile} compiles one AST expression, while
@code{Sec_5_5.compile_program} compiles the items of a complete unit.
@code{Sec_5_5.run} admits no source itself: it executes a checked program.
The compiler returns typed instructions with register-use metadata.
@code{Sec_5_5.statements_text} renders those instructions for inspection;
the machine does not parse that text.

The compiler and evaluators use the same guest AST.  The direct and
explicit-control evaluators interpret checked programs.  The compiler emits
instructions for the 5.1 register machine.  All paths must preserve the
guest program's results, output, errors, and effects.  Instruction counts
and stack activity are machine-specific observations, not guest behavior.

