@subsection Structure of the Compiler

The compiler starts from the typed AST produced by source admission.  The
same AST is consumed by the direct evaluator, the explicit-control
evaluator, and this compiler.  Code generation walks that tree and returns
a sequence of typed register-machine instructions.

@code{Check.check} is the source boundary.  It parses one complete unit,
checks the closed guest grammar, and verifies its types with the pinned
compiler before it returns a program.  @code{Sec_5_5.compile} accepts one
AST expression.
@code{Sec_5_5.compile_program} accepts the items of a checked unit.  The
compiler does not read source text.  This low-level example builds an AST
directly; source programs normally pass through @code{Check.check}:

@example ocaml
module A = Sicp_common.Ast
module C = Sicp_ch5.Sec_5_5

let expression = A.apply (A.var "f") [ A.scalar (A.Int 42) ]
let state = C.new_state ()
let code = C.compile state expression "val" C.Next
@end example

@noindent
The private @code{compile_step} dispatches on @code{Ast.view}.  The live
expression forms include @code{Ast.Scalar}, @code{Ast.Var},
@code{Ast.Let}, @code{Ast.Fun}, @code{Ast.Apply}, @code{Ast.If},
@code{Ast.Match}, @code{Ast.Sequence}, and the Boolean, arithmetic,
comparison, reference, tuple, record, and constructor forms.  There are no
AST cases for quotation, variable assignment, or @code{cond}.  The
@code{Ast.Assign} case is assignment through a reference.

@example ocaml
let rec compile_step ctx state e target linkage =
  match Ast.view e with
  | Ast.Scalar s ->
    end_with_linkage
      ctx
      linkage
      (make_instruction_sequence
         []
         [ target ]
         [ M.Assign
             (target,
              M.Const
                (W.V
                   (Sicp_ch4.Sec_4_1.scalar_value s))) ])
  | Ast.Nil ->
    end_with_linkage
      ctx
      linkage
      (make_instruction_sequence
         []
         [ target ]
         [ M.Assign (target, M.Const (W.V Value.nil)) ])
  | Ast.Var _ ->
    end_with_linkage
      ctx
      linkage
      (assign_op [ "env" ] target
         "lookup-variable-value" [ exp e; M.Reg "env" ])
  | Ast.Fun (_, body) ->
    compile_lambda ctx state e body target linkage
  | Ast.If (c, t, f) ->
    compile_if ctx state c t f target linkage
  | Ast.And (a, b) ->
    compile_if ctx state a b
      (Ast.scalar (Ast.Bool false)) target linkage
  | Ast.Or (a, b) ->
    compile_if ctx state a
      (Ast.scalar (Ast.Bool true)) b target linkage
  | Ast.Sequence (a, b) ->
    ctx.pres
      [ "env"; "continue" ]
      (ctx.self state a target Next)
      (ctx.self state b target linkage)
  | Ast.Let (false, bindings, body) ->
    let names =
      List.map
        (fun (b : Ast.binding) ->
           Option.value b.name ~default:"_")
        bindings
    in
    let rhss =
      List.map (fun (b : Ast.binding) -> b.rhs) bindings
    in
    compile_application ctx state
      (Ast.fun_ names body) rhss target linkage
  | Ast.Let (true, bindings, body) ->
    append_2_sequences
      (compile_rec_group ctx state e bindings)
      (ctx.self state body target linkage)
  | Ast.Match (scrutinee, cases) ->
    compile_match ctx state scrutinee cases target linkage
  | Ast.Apply (fn, args) ->
    compile_application ctx state fn args target linkage
  | Ast.Arith (_, a, b)
  | Ast.Compare (_, a, b)
  | Ast.Concat (a, b)
  | Ast.Cons (a, b)
  | Ast.Assign (a, b) ->
    end_with_linkage
      ctx
      linkage
      (ctx.pres
         [ "env" ]
         (ctx.self state a "arg1" Next)
         (ctx.pres
            [ "arg1" ]
            (ctx.self state b "arg2" Next)
            (assign_op
               [ "arg1"; "arg2" ]
               target
               "apply-binary"
               [ exp e; M.Reg "arg1"; M.Reg "arg2" ])))
  | Ast.Not a
  | Ast.Neg a
  | Ast.Deref a
  | Ast.Make_ref a
  | Ast.Field (a, _) ->
    end_with_linkage
      ctx
      linkage
      (append_2_sequences
         (ctx.self state a "val" Next)
         (assign_op
            [ "val" ] target "apply-unary"
            [ exp e; M.Reg "val" ]))
  | Ast.Tuple parts ->
    compile_build ctx state e parts target linkage
  | Ast.Construct (_, fields) ->
    compile_build ctx state e fields target linkage
  | Ast.Record fields ->
    compile_build ctx state e
      (List.map snd fields) target linkage
@end example

@noindent
The aliases in this private source are @code{Ast} for the checked AST,
@code{M} for the typed instruction constructors, and @code{W} for
explicit-control words.  The public callers use @code{Sec_5_5.compile}
or @code{Sec_5_5.compile_program}.

@subsubheading Targets and linkages

A code generator takes an expression, a target register, and a linkage.
The target names the register that receives the expression's value.
The public linkage type has three cases:

@example ocaml
type linkage =
  | Next
  | Return
  | Goto_label of string
@end example

@noindent
@code{Next} continues at the following instruction.
@code{Return} jumps through the @code{continue} register.
@code{Goto_label} jumps to a named label.  This call compiles the integer
literal @code{5} to @code{val} and continues at the next instruction:

@example ocaml
module A = Sicp_common.Ast
module C = Sicp_ch5.Sec_5_5

let state = C.new_state ()
let five = A.scalar (A.Int 5)
let code = C.compile state five "val" C.Next
@end example

@noindent
The compiled expression leaves its result in @code{val}.  The same
expression with @code{C.Return} ends with the return transfer.
@code{C.Goto_label label} selects a named continuation.

@subsubheading Instruction sequences and stack usage

A sequence records the registers it needs, the registers it modifies, and
its typed statements.  The public type is @code{Sec_5_5.seq}; its fields
are @code{needs}, @code{modifies}, and @code{statements}.  Sequential
composition uses this metadata to avoid saves when an earlier sequence
does not overwrite a value needed later.

@example ocaml
module C = Sicp_ch5.Sec_5_5

let first = C.empty_instruction_sequence
let second = C.empty_instruction_sequence
let combined =
  C.preserving [ "env"; "continue" ] first second
@end example

@noindent
@code{preserving} saves a listed register only when @code{first} modifies
it and @code{second} needs its incoming value.  It then restores the
register before the second sequence.  The instruction data remains typed
through compilation and assembly.  @code{statements_text} is a renderer
for inspection; its output is not the object program.

@quotation
