@node	5.5.2, 5.5.3, 5.5.1, 5.5
@subsection Compiling Expressions

The compiler handles the expression variants admitted by the OCaml subset.
Its input is an AST node, not a string of source text.  The compiler uses
the same scalar, variable, function, application, and control-flow forms
as the evaluators.

@subsubheading Compiling linkage code

The linkage determines where an expression continues after it leaves its
result in the target register.  @code{Next} falls through,
@code{Return} transfers through @code{continue}, and @code{Goto_label}
transfers to a named label.  The compiler appends the selected transfer
to the expression's instruction sequence.  @code{Sec_5_5.compile} exposes
this choice directly:

@example ocaml
module A = Sicp_common.Ast
module C = Sicp_ch5.Sec_5_5

let expression = A.scalar (A.Int 5)
let code = C.compile (C.new_state ()) expression "val" C.Return
@end example

@subsubheading Compiling simple expressions

The AST represents scalar literals with @code{Ast.Scalar}, the empty
list with @code{Ast.Nil}, and variable references with @code{Ast.Var}.
The compiler places scalar values in the requested register.  A variable
reference emits a lookup using the current @code{env}.  The language has
no quotation form, so there is no quotation compiler.

The AST builders can also create an application node for a compiler
probe.  Source programs normally obtain the same node through admission:

@example ocaml
module A = Sicp_common.Ast
module C = Sicp_ch5.Sec_5_5

let application =
  A.apply (A.var "f")
    [ A.scalar (A.Int 10); A.scalar (A.Int 32) ]
let code = C.compile (C.new_state ()) application "val" C.Next
@end example

@noindent
The remaining compound forms have their own AST variants.  A
@code{let} group is @code{Ast.Let}; a function is @code{Ast.Fun};
an application is @code{Ast.Apply}.  Guest bindings are immutable.
Mutation is explicit: @code{Ast.Make_ref} allocates a cell,
@code{Ast.Deref} reads it, and @code{Ast.Assign} updates it.  Assignment
does not rebind a variable name.

@subsubheading Compiling conditional expressions

The compiler handles @code{Ast.If} with a test, two branches, and a join
label.  The guest subset also has short-circuit @code{Ast.And} and
@code{Ast.Or} expressions.  The compiler lowers them through the same
conditional path, so it evaluates the right operand only when the left
operand requires it.  The test leaves its Boolean result in @code{val};
the selected branch leaves the expression result in the requested target.

The guest language has no @code{cond} form.  It also has no implicit
conversion from a general value to a condition.  The admission checker
has already established the Boolean type before the compiler runs.

@subsubheading Compiling sequences

A source sequence @code{first; second} is represented by
@code{Ast.Sequence}.  The compiler emits the first expression before the
second and leaves the second expression's value in the target register.
Register preservation protects the environment or return destination
when a later expression needs it.

@subsubheading Compiling @code{fun} expressions

A function expression is @code{Ast.Fun} with a parameter list and body.
The compiler creates a compiled closure that retains its definition
environment.  Its entry code binds the arguments in a new environment
and then compiles the body with return linkage.

A non-recursive @code{Ast.Let} compiles as a function application.
A recursive @code{Ast.Let} allocates the group's cells before compiling
and filling its right-hand sides.  This supports mutually recursive
bindings without rewriting them as variable assignment.

@node	5.5.3, 5.5.4, 5.5.2, 5.5
@subsection Compiling Combinations

An application node contains an operator expression and its operand
expressions.  The compiler first compiles the operator to @code{proc}.
It then evaluates operands from left to right and builds @code{argl}.
Finally, it calls @code{compile_procedure_call}:

@example ocaml
module C = Sicp_ch5.Sec_5_5

let state = C.new_state ()
let call = C.compile_procedure_call state "val" C.Return
@end example

@noindent
This public entry point compiles the call after the machine has placed the
procedure in @code{proc} and arguments in @code{argl}.  In a full
application, the compiler constructs both registers from the
@code{Ast.Apply} node.  It preserves @code{env}, @code{proc},
@code{argl}, and @code{continue} when later code needs their incoming
values.

@subsubheading Applying procedures

The call sequence tests whether a primitive can run inline with the exact
argument count.  Otherwise, it transfers to the shared
@code{compiled-apply} routine.  That routine applies primitives and
compiled closures, and it handles partial and excess arguments.  The
compiler does not build separate textual controller fragments for these
paths.  It builds typed instructions from the instruction constructors of
@ref{5.1}.

Several operators have dedicated AST forms.  @code{Ast.Arith},
@code{Ast.Compare}, @code{Ast.Concat}, @code{Ast.Cons}, and
@code{Ast.Assign} compile through the binary-operation path.
@code{Ast.Not}, @code{Ast.Neg}, @code{Ast.Deref}, @code{Ast.Make_ref},
and @code{Ast.Field} use the unary-operation path.  @code{Ast.Tuple},
@code{Ast.Construct}, and @code{Ast.Record} evaluate their fields and
use the value-building path.  A call through @code{Ast.Apply} uses the
general procedure path.  The checker has already rejected ill-typed
operands.

@subsubheading Applying compiled procedures

A compiled closure stores its entry label, parameter names, and captured
environment.@footnote{@anchor{Footnote 323} We need
machine operations to implement a data structure for representing compiled
procedures, analogous to the structure for compound procedures described in
@ref{4.1.3}.  This edition builds the procedure object with the
@code{make-compiled-procedure} operation, which the shared runtime turns
into a compiled-closure value that keeps the entry label, parameter names,
and captured environment.}  At the entry label, the shared runtime binds
arguments to
parameters and evaluates the function body in the captured environment.
A call with the exact arity transfers directly to that entry.  A call with
too few arguments creates a partial closure; a call with excess arguments
applies the remaining arguments to the first result.

A call in return position can reuse the current @code{continue}
destination.  Exact-arity tail calls therefore need no extra return save.
If a compiled procedure receives excess arguments, the runtime saves
@code{continue} and the remaining argument list.  It resumes after the
call and applies those arguments to the first result.

Stack allocation and garbage collection remain competing runtime
strategies; see @ref{Hanson 1990}, @ref{Appel 1987}, and
@ref{Miller and Rozas 1994}.

@node	5.5.4, 5.5.5, 5.5.3, 5.5
@subsection Combining Instruction Sequences

A compiler sequence stores its register needs, its register modifications,
and typed instructions.  When two sequences run in order, registers written
by the first sequence no longer need their incoming values in the combined
sequence.  Its needs are therefore the needs of the first sequence together
with the needs of the second that the first does not modify.

The public combiners expose sequential composition, alternatives, and
non-executed function bodies:

@example ocaml
module C = Sicp_ch5.Sec_5_5

let empty = C.empty_instruction_sequence
let first = empty
let second = empty
let ordered = C.append_sequences [ first; second ]
let alternatives =
  C.parallel_instruction_sequences first second
let protected = C.preserving [ "env"; "continue" ] first second
let closure_code = C.tack_on_instruction_sequence first second
@end example

@noindent
Sequential composition carries forward the register metadata.  Alternative
branches combine both branches' needs, because either branch may read an
incoming value.  @code{preserving} adds @code{Save} and @code{Restore}
instructions only when the first sequence modifies a register that the
second needs.  @code{tack_on_instruction_sequence} appends a function body's
instructions without merging its needs into the closure-construction path.

These operations are in @code{Sec_5_5.mli}.  They work on typed statement
lists, not rendered controller strings.  @code{statements_text} is useful
for reading those statements, but assembly and execution use the original
instruction values.

@node	5.5.5, 5.5.6, 5.5.4, 5.5
@subsection An Example of Compiled Code

This complete OCaml-subset unit defines recursive factorial and prints its
result:

@example ocaml
let rec factorial n =
  if n = 1 then 1 else n * factorial (n - 1)
let () = print_int (factorial 5); print_newline ()
@end example

@noindent
Admission checks the source before compilation.  The host compiler API
accepts the checked items and returns a sequence of typed instructions:

@example ocaml
module C = Sicp_ch5.Sec_5_5
module Check = Sicp_common.Check

let factorial_source =
  "let rec factorial n =\n\
   if n = 1 then 1 else n * factorial (n - 1)\n\
   let () = print_int (factorial 5); print_newline ()"
;;
let checked = Check.check ~filename:"factorial.ml" factorial_source
;;
let inspection =
  match checked with
  | Error diagnostic -> Check.diagnostic_to_string diagnostic
  | Ok program ->
    let sequence =
      C.compile_program (C.new_state ()) (Check.items program)
    in
    C.statements_text sequence
;;
@end example

@noindent
The renderer is a view of the instruction values.  @code{load} assembles
the typed sequence, and @code{run} compiles and executes the checked
program.  The renderer output is never parsed as machine instructions.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Figure 5.17}Figure 5.17:} The compiled-program path:
guest source, admission, typed code generation, and machine execution.
@end quotation

@noindent
To inspect the code for an expression directly, use @code{compile}.
To inspect a whole unit, use @code{compile_program}.  Both APIs return
the same instruction-sequence type:

@example ocaml
module C = Sicp_ch5.Sec_5_5
module Check = Sicp_common.Check

let trace =
  match checked with
  | Error diagnostic -> Check.diagnostic_to_string diagnostic
  | Ok program ->
    C.statements_text
      (C.compile_program (C.new_state ()) (Check.items program))
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Figure 5.18}Figure 5.18:} Reading the typed factorial
instruction sequence through @code{statements_text}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 5.33}Exercise 5.33:} Compare the compiled
statements for these two complete guest units.  Explain how their
expression trees change the call order and register saves:

@example ocaml
let rec factorial n =
  if n = 1 then 1 else n * factorial (n - 1)
let () = print_int (factorial 5)
@end example

@example ocaml
let rec factorial_alt n =
  if n = 1 then 1 else factorial_alt (n - 1) * n
let () = print_int (factorial_alt 5)
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
