@subsection Lexical Addressing

The OCaml subset has lexical scope, and the checker resolves every name
before execution.  The compiler does not yet turn a local name into a
numeric lexical address.  Its @code{Ast.Var} case emits a
@code{lookup-variable-value} operation that searches the runtime
environment by name.

Consider this guest unit:

@example ocaml
let outer = 3
let make x =
  let local = x + outer in
  fun y -> local + y
let answer = make 4 5
@end example

@noindent
The closure captures the environment where @code{make} creates it.
Function application and @code{let} create environment frames.  The
compiler preserves these semantics, but it does not calculate frame
depths or slot displacements.  A lexical-address optimization would need
to keep those compile-time frames aligned with the environments made by
the runtime.

