@node	5.5.7, References, 5.4, 5.5
@subsection Interfacing Compiled Code to the Evaluator

The compiler and both evaluators accept the same checked program, but each
run creates its own runtime state.  Admission happens once at the host
boundary:

@example ocaml
module Check = Sicp_common.Check
let checked =
  Check.check ~filename:"factorial.ml" factorial_source
@end example

@noindent
For a successful @code{Check.program}, compare the direct evaluator, the
explicit-control evaluator, and the compiler:

@example ocaml
module Direct = Sicp_ch4.Sec_4_1
module Controlled = Sicp_ch5.Sec_5_4
module Compiled = Sicp_ch5.Sec_5_5

let runs =
  match checked with
  | Error diagnostic -> Error diagnostic
  | Ok program ->
    let direct = Direct.run ~emit:print_string program in
    let controlled = Controlled.run ~emit:print_string program in
    let compiled = Compiled.run ~emit:print_string program in
    Ok (direct, controlled, compiled)
@end example

@noindent
Compare native OCaml execution with these engines only for observations
the host language specifies.  Avoid side effects in argument positions
whose order the host leaves unspecified.

Compare output, result values, and guest error categories.  The direct
and explicit-control evaluators use interpreted closures.  The compiler
uses compiled closures with entry labels.  The public run functions do not
provide a bridge between those procedure representations.

@code{run_stats} reports the number of executed instructions for compiled
execution.  To inspect stack use, compile the checked items and pass the
sequence to @code{load}.  Start the returned machine with
@code{Sec_5_1.start}; then read @code{Sec_5_1.executed} and
@code{Sec_5_1.stack_statistics}.  Counts describe one engine; they are
not the guest behavior contract.

Interpretation evaluates the checked AST through guest operations.
Compilation chooses register instructions before execution.  Both paths
must preserve guest results, output, errors, and effects.  The compiler
can remove machine work only when it proves that the guest behavior stays
the same.

Safety checks cost instructions.  A compiler should remove a check only
when it proves that the check is unnecessary.  The historical
@abbr{UNIX}(tm) finger daemon failure exploited a missing input-buffer
check; see @ref{Spafford 1989}.  Measure safe code generation.  Do not
remove a required guest check to improve a counter.

To port this execution model to another host, implement the explicit-control
machine and its runtime, or lower checked programs and provide the
operations and storage they need.  Exercises 5.51 and 5.52 ask readers
to explore the C path.  The OCaml compiler interface itself returns typed
register-machine instructions, not C code.

